Head and Neck Cancer (Squamous Cell Carcinoma)
Opthalmology & ENT
Non-healing mucosal ulcer, Fixed cervical lymphadenopathy, Unilateral referred otalgia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-022
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting unilateral ear pain, severe difficulty swallowing, and a rapidly expanding neck lump.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with a rapidly enlarging, rock-hard neck mass, severe unilateral otalgia, and progressive dysphagia leading to dehydration. Urgent assessment was necessary to rule out an impending airway obstruction, a rapidly expanding deep neck space abscess, or acute cranial nerve compromise, and to manage the severe, unrelenting pain preventing oral hydration prior to morning services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My left ear is killing me, I can barely swallow water without choking, and this rock on my neck is getting bigger every day."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 3-month history of a painless, persistent, non-healing ulcer on the left lateral border of the tongue. Over the last 4 weeks, developed severe, constant left-sided otalgia and progressive dysphagia with significant odynophagia, resulting in a 6kg unintended weight loss. Notes a firm, progressively enlarging lump in the left neck. Endorses chronic, unrelenting hoarseness and halitosis. Heavy smoking history (40 pack-years) and daily alcohol consumption.
Functional Status: Weakened from poor oral intake; ambulating with difficulty.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; significant risk of severe dehydration and malnutrition.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 95 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 60 Kg (Reported recent loss)
General: Cachectic, frail, speaking with a muffled, hoarse voice. Distinct foul odor (halitosis) present.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal examination reveals a 3cm, fungating, indurated, and ulcerated lesion on the left lateral border of the tongue extending to the floor of the mouth. The tongue is fixed and deviates to the left on protrusion. Left tympanic membrane is completely pristine and normal, indicating referred otalgia. Neck examination reveals a 5cm, rock-hard, fixed, and indurated left cervical lymph node mass (Level II). No stridor or use of accessory muscles, but pooling of saliva is noted in the oral cavity.
Environmental Observations: Ashtrays full of cigarette butts; empty alcohol bottles visible.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma (Advanced) - Status: Acute deterioration
Differentials:

Acute Otitis Media - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The tympanic membrane being completely normal on otoscopy; the severe ear pain is referred pain from the tongue/pharynx via cranial nerves.

Deep Neck Space Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The neck mass being rock-hard, non-fluctuant, and fixed, without overlying severe acute erythema or high systemic fever.

Benign Aphthous Ulcer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The lesion being large, fungating, fixed, and non-healing for months, accompanied by massive fixed cervical lymphadenopathy and cranial nerve involvement (tongue deviation).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Cachexia, solitary living, complex malignancy)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in a state of subacute physiological collapse driven by severe referred pain, dysphagia, and a rapidly enlarging malignant-appearing mass. Urgent face-to-face assessment was absolutely critical to physically evaluate the airway for impending obstruction, rule out an acute expanding abscess, and diagnose the source of the profound otalgia. By identifying the primary fungating tongue tumor and fixed nodal metastasis, the immediate clinical priority shifted from treating a presumed simple ear infection to aggressive, immediate pain control to allow hydration, and arranging an expedited, fast-track two-week wait referral for definitive oncological staging and biopsy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Morphine solution (liquid) for severe pain to bypass the difficulty of swallowing solid tablets. Generated a 'Fast-Track' suspected cancer referral to the Head and Neck ENT/Oncology multidisciplinary team.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain hydration despite the severe odynophagia, the patient must take their liquid morphine exactly 30 minutes before attempting to drink high-calorie nutritional supplements. The reliable, timed reduction in severe swallowing pain provides immense positive reinforcement, ensuring compliance with the heavy analgesic regimen and preventing critical dehydration and further cachexia while awaiting specialist intervention.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral for community palliative/oncology nursing support.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handed over directly to the fast-track ENT oncology pathway; GP review in 48 hours for clinical welfare check.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops sudden difficulty breathing, noisy breathing (stridor), or absolutely cannot swallow their own saliva resulting in continuous drooling.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis requires formal histopathological biopsy; clinical suspicion for malignancy is extremely high.
Clinical Course: The pain and swelling will not improve without definitive specialist treatment. The immediate goal is strict pain control to allow you to drink and maintain strength for the upcoming hospital tests.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you start struggling for breath, or if you cannot swallow your own spit and start choking, call 000 immediately as your airway may be closing.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The lump in your mouth and neck are highly suspicious for a serious cancer. The ear pain is actually a 'false signal' sent by the nerves in your throat. You need urgent biopsies at the hospital.
Actionable Steps: Use the liquid morphine exactly 30 minutes before meals. Drink high-calorie liquid meals (e.g., Ensure/Sustagen) rather than attempting solid food. Stop smoking immediately.

